package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * # Math — numeric utilities + the device math intrinsics
 *
 * `Math` is the one stop for scalar and vector numeric functions. It has two
 * kinds of members:
 *
 * 1. **Declared, method-level-templated** — `max`, `min`, `clamp` below. Each is
 *    monomorphized per `T` at the call site (zero dispatch cost):
 *    ```
 *    int32   a = Math.max(3, 7);          // -> max<int32>   = 7
 *    float64 b = Math.clamp(1.5, 0.0, 1.0); // -> clamp<float64> = 1.0
 *    ```
 * 2. **Compiler intrinsics** — `sqrt`, `sin`, `cos`, `pow`, … are not declared
 *    here; the compiler intercepts the call and emits the right instruction
 *    (host *and* inside an `@Kernel`). They are documented below so this file is
 *    the single source of the Math surface.
 *
 * ## Intrinsic functions
 *
 * **Native (lower directly on every backend — no device library):**
 * `sqrt`, `floor`, `ceil`, `trunc`, `round`, `abs`, `fma(a,b,c)` (= `a*b+c`),
 * plus the scalar `min`/`max` above.
 * ```
 * float32 h = Math.sqrt(x*x + y*y);
 * float32 m = Math.fma(a, b, c);        // fused multiply-add
 * ```
 *
 * **Transcendentals (B2 increment 2):** `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `acos`,
 * `atan`, `atan2(y,x)`, `exp`, `exp2`, `log`, `log2`, `log10`, `pow(x,y)`,
 * `rsqrt(x)` (= `1/sqrt(x)`). In a kernel these realize per backend — CPU
 * `libm`, Vulkan `OpExtInst GLSL.std.450`, AMD `ocml` (`__ocml_*`), NVIDIA
 * `libdevice` (`__nv_*`):
 * ```
 * @Kernel
 * public static void rotate(GpuBuffer<float32> out, float32 angle, uint32 n) {
 *     uint32 i = Thread.globalIdX();
 *     if (i < n) { out[i] = Math.cos(angle) - Math.sin(angle); }
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ## Vectorized forms
 *
 * Any of the intrinsics applies **element-wise** to a `Vector<T,N>`:
 * ```
 * Vector<float32,4> v  = Vector<float32,4>(0.0f, 0.5f, 1.0f, 2.0f);
 * Vector<float32,4> s  = Math.sin(v);     // (sin v.x, sin v.y, sin v.z, sin v.w)
 * Vector<float32,4> sq = Math.sqrt(v);    // element-wise sqrt
 * ```
 * (On AMD/NVIDIA the per-lane device-library call is scalarized automatically.)
 *
 * ## Precision: `@FastMath`
 *
 * Annotate a `@Kernel` with `@FastMath` to relax IEEE FP for its whole body —
 * the backend may fuse multiply-adds, use reciprocals, and pick approximate
 * transcendentals (the LLVM fast-math flags). Opt-in; it trades precision for
 * speed:
 * ```
 * @Kernel @FastMath
 * public static void saxpy(GpuBuffer<float32> y, GpuBuffer<float32> x,
 *                          float32 a, uint32 n) {
 *     uint32 i = Thread.globalIdX();
 *     if (i < n) { y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i]; }   // may contract to one fma
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * **v1 limitation (declared templates only):** the `T` bound on `max`/`min`/
 * `clamp` (`Comparable`) isn't enforced yet — the body uses `>`/`<`, which work
 * for built-in primitives but won't link for user classes until operator
 * overloading flows through method-level template specialization.
 *
 * @see docs/MaskSelect.md      branchless per-lane conditionals
 * @see docs/stdlib/MethodLevelTemplate.md   the templating mechanism
 */
public class Math {
    /**
     * The larger of `a` and `b`. Per-`T` monomorphization; non-virtual (it is
     * `static` and method-templated).
     * ```
     * Math.max(3, 7)       // 7   (max<int32>)
     * Math.max(1.5, 2.5)   // 2.5 (max<float64>)
     * ```
     * (For an element-wise vector max use `a.max(b)` on a `Vector<T,N>`.)
     */
    public static T max<T>(T a, T b) {
        if (a > b) { return a; }
        return b;
    }

    /**
     * The smaller of `a` and `b` (per-`T`, like {@link #max}).
     * ```
     * Math.min(3, 7)   // 3
     * ```
     */
    public static T min<T>(T a, T b) {
        if (a < b) { return a; }
        return b;
    }

    /**
     * `x` clamped to `[lo, hi]`. Built from `min` + `max` (themselves
     * method-templated) so the one declaration specializes for every `T`.
     * ```
     * Math.clamp(15, 0, 10)   // 10  (above the range)
     * Math.clamp(-5, 0, 10)   // 0   (below)
     * Math.clamp(5,  0, 10)   // 5   (in range)
     * ```
     */
    public static T clamp<T>(T x, T lo, T hi) {
        return Math.max(lo, Math.min(hi, x));
    }
}
